WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.939
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.939 --> 00:00:06.390
We like to define things by their age and
sometimes it’s a very important piece of

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:11.310
information, especially when it comes to stuff
like dating, safety equipment or that jar

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.350
at the back of your fridge; don’t open that,
you will regret it.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:19.120
When it comes to estimating the age of a person,
we mainly go on their appearance but when

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.410
it comes to ideas, products or technology,
then we often think about things associated

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:25.410
with them.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:29.750
For instance, you would think a video games
company would have started no more than 50

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.759
years ago, because of how new the industry
is.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.969
But this doesn’t always work.

00:00:34.969 --> 00:00:39.449
So today, just like that time you swiped right
on your friend’s grandma, we’re looking

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:44.450
at things much older than you expected.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:52.620
Mario, Link, Donkey Kong, Samus Aran; these
are the names of some of the greatest video

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:57.940
game characters of all time, as well as many
pets and probably a few unfortunate children

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.719
whose parents thought it would be a cool name.

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:06.320
As one of the very earliest video game companies,
obviously you know Nintendo has been around

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.310
for a fair while but, very surprisingly, they
were founded in 1889.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:17.970
That’s right, a video game company was founded
over 120 years ago.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:22.200
Obviously there was no personal computing
back then, there weren’t even TV screens.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.920
So was Mario based on a real-life game where
men dressed-up as plumbers would crush turtles?

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:34.060
Well Nintendo actually started out as a playing
card company, set up by Fusajiro Yamauchi.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.180
In fact, they still make playing cards today.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:42.470
In 1956, Hiroshi Yamauchi, the grandson of
the founder, went to visit the United States

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:44.110
Playing Card Company.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:47.990
He saw that despite them being the biggest
card company in the world, they were still

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.550
just in a small office.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:53.600
This didn’t fit the grand ambitions he had
for Nintendo.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.890
He tried out many ventures and over the following
years Nintendo ran a taxi company, a TV network,

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:03.750
a food brand and even a series of love hotels.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:09.319
So Zelda could have been a type of rice and
Perfect Dark could have been a very gloomy

00:02:09.319 --> 00:02:10.409
erotic penthouse.

00:02:10.409 --> 00:02:17.370
But it was Nintendo’s shift to toys in 1966
that got them where they are today.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.849
They saw the potential of the light gun and
how people could interact with a screen.

00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:29.090
This led to the hand-held Game &amp; Watch series
and then, in 1983, the Famicom and NES.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:34.489
Interestingly, Mario was actually named after
Mario Segale, the landlord at the American

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:39.530
Nintendo office, who allegedly let them pay
their rent late when they were struggling.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.819
He also ate magic mushrooms and saved a princess
from a giant evil turtle, but this is probably

00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:47.160
just a coincidence.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:51.220
Emoticons are so common that your mum is likely
to just text you “chicken leg, snowflake,

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.719
angry face” when she’s mad that your dinner
is getting cold.

00:02:55.719 --> 00:02:59.560
But the first emoticons are over 150 years
old.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.779
Morse code had its own form of abbreviation,
with certain numbers being given a meaning

00:03:04.779 --> 00:03:07.489
so as to save on unnecessary tapping time.

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:11.849
1 was “wait a minute”,
13 was “I don’t understand”

00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:17.659
and 22 was “Love and Kisses” although
this became 88 at some point later on, maybe

00:03:17.659 --> 00:03:20.760
we needed four times as much love and kisses
in the world.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.779
These aren’t really emoticons though.

00:03:22.779 --> 00:03:28.060
A transcript of an Abraham Lincoln speech
in the New York Times from 1862, contains

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:34.010
the line "(applause and laughter ;)" like
an inside joke for Lincoln.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.040
There is some debate as to whether this was
intentional or just a typo.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.760
There was no autocorrect in the time of Abracadabra
Lincoln, so there could have been a lot of

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.560
milkshakes in his spleen…

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.560
I mean mistakes in his speech.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:52.189
There is no doubt about the emoticons in Puck
magazine in 1881 though since they are clearly

00:03:52.189 --> 00:03:53.260
labelled.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.430
These may be the first documented emoticons.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:02.189
Scott Fahlman, an early computer scientist,
is credited as creating the happy and sad

00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:06.909
emoticons we know today :-) :-( since he described
them on a message board on 19th September

00:04:06.909 --> 00:04:08.540
1982.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:14.120
The smiley was to indicate jokes but he also
mentioned “it is probably more economical

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.009
to mark things that are NOT jokes, given current
trends.”

00:04:18.009 --> 00:04:21.169
It’s a laugh a minute down in IT apparently.

00:04:21.169 --> 00:04:27.500
Speaking of paperwork, it used to be a difficult
thing to get to people, before the world went

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.500
digital.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.780
And if you wanted something faster than relying
on the postman, then you turned to the fax

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:33.790
machine.

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:38.740
Through the 80s and 90s, fax was really your
only option to send reports, contracts and

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.590
offensive doodles of your boss, across the
country in an instant.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:47.860
Although it wasn’t until the mid-60s that
commercial machines came onto the market,

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:55.710
the first ever fax was made all the way back
in 1843, over 30 years before the telephone.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:05:00.990
And like Alexander Graham Bell, it was another
Scotsman, Alexander Bain, who invented the

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:02.210
fax machine.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:06.300
Perhaps there is something about the cold,
grey Scottish climate that made them find

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.460
any way possible to avoid leaving the house.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:14.020
The first commercial service was with Giovanni
Caselli’s Pantelegraph.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:17.540
Senders would use non-conducting ink and write
on a sheet of tin.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:21.650
A metal stylus would move across the sheet
and would register the current, which would

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.669
cut out every time it passed over the ink.

00:05:24.669 --> 00:05:29.740
The pattern of the electrical current was
sent via telegram and a machine on the other

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:34.830
end would print it out by using paper soaked
in chemicals, which would react and stain

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.389
the page when a current passed through it.

00:05:37.389 --> 00:05:42.030
The biggest difficulty was synching up the
machines, so they used large, heavy pendulums

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:47.229
to stop the receiving machine from only printing
the first half of the document, and your girlfriend

00:05:47.229 --> 00:05:51.229
receiving a fax saying “My dearest Susanne,
I’ve found someone else.”

00:05:51.229 --> 00:05:56.250
Yeah… good luck explaining to her that it
should have ended with “to fix the leak

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:57.919
on my roof”.

00:05:57.919 --> 00:06:02.539
Modern communication has made a lot of old
tech redundant and the language and form is

00:06:02.539 --> 00:06:03.570
constantly changing.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:09.270
But as with the emoticon, some of the things
that we associate solely with internet culture,

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.680
actual pre-date it by quite some time.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.970
And that is very true of the @ symbol.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:20.639
The earliest record we have of it is from
1345, in a Bulgarian translation of a Greek

00:06:20.639 --> 00:06:21.639
chronicle.

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:25.530
But its usage was relatively common place
when recording trade.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:30.669
One theory is that it started as an ‘a’
inside an ‘e’ to denote “each at”

00:06:30.669 --> 00:06:31.840
rather than just “at”.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:37.070
If you wrote “10 apples at $2” it might
be confusing whether it was $2 for all of

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:40.530
them, or $2 each i.e. $20 total.

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:43.000
This symbol could have been used to avoid
that confusion.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:50.099
But others think it was for monks writing
the Latin “ad”, since they were always

00:06:50.099 --> 00:06:54.919
looking to abbreviate to save on ink and time,
as everything was done by hand.

00:06:54.919 --> 00:07:00.349
It was also commonly used in Spain and Portugal
from the 16th century to mean arroba, a unit

00:07:00.349 --> 00:07:01.349
of weight.

00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:04.980
These days, many languages have a different
nickname for it.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:10.430
The Koreans call it snail, the Danish call
it elephant’s trunk, the Slovaks call it

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:14.940
pickled herring and the Dutch sometimes call
it little monkey’s testicle.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.410
You do not want to see Holland’s browsing
history.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:23.920
Many great linguists have come from the second
oldest university in the world; The university

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:24.920
of Oxford.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:28.229
It has been around in some form since 1096.

00:07:28.229 --> 00:07:35.069
That’s only 30 years after the Battle of
Hastings in 1066, you know the only date you

00:07:35.069 --> 00:07:37.139
can actually remember from history class.

00:07:37.139 --> 00:07:42.110
And here’s a fact that will destroy everything
you thought you knew about history, Oxford

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:49.150
University is actually older than the Aztec
Empire, which began in 1325.

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:53.760
Only the University of Bologna has existed
for longer, being founded in 1088.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.349
Although Bologna is older, Oxford is surely
the more famous, a name that is respected

00:07:58.349 --> 00:07:59.749
around the globe.

00:07:59.749 --> 00:08:04.720
Its alumni include Sir Walter Raleigh, who
led a lot of the English colonisation of North

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:10.310
America, the great architect Sir Christopher
Wren, actor Emma Watson, that bloke who created

00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:17.009
the internet Sir Tim Berners-Lee and authors
JR Tolkien, Lewis Carroll, Oscar Wilde, C.S.

00:08:17.009 --> 00:08:19.410
Lewis and Dr. Seuss among others.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:22.889
Now here’s a snack that is far older than
you’d expect.

00:08:22.889 --> 00:08:26.539
I’m not talking about that egg sandwich
that you bought in a moment of madness from

00:08:26.539 --> 00:08:29.610
the gas station, it’s popcorn.

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:34.849
Corn was domesticated in Mexico some 9,000
years ago and we have found evidence of popcorn

00:08:34.849 --> 00:08:41.800
that is 5,600 years old, made by Native Americans
down in New Mexico, so it’s a true American

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:42.800
classic.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:48.220
The oldest existing snack brand in the states
is Baker’s Chocolate, founded by John Hannon

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:51.450
and Dr. James Baker in 1764.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:56.760
It was originally called Hannon’s Best Chocolate
but when Hannon disappeared on a sailing trip

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:00.600
in 1779, Baker took his chance.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:05.040
After all, life is like a box of chocolates;
you eat them all yourself as soon as you get

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.560
the opportunity.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:11.320
And our final item also comes from an old
snack; spam.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:15.500
But we’re not talking about the disgusting,
pre-cooked canned meat, that became popular

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:16.920
in the post war period.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:21.200
No, we’re talking about the never ending
stream of annoying messages.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:24.750
It’s no coincidence that they share the
same name though.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:29.850
The idea comes from an old Monty Python sketch
in a café, where nearly every item on the

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:34.290
menu includes spam, with some baked beans
thrown in there somewhere.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:38.730
When early internet chat rooms began, some
users would repeatedly type “spam” in

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:42.970
order to shift a line of text off-screen,
since you wouldn’t have the ability to scroll

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:45.410
through conversations like we do now.

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:50.850
So it became the common word for sending out
unwanted messages to many people, with no

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:53.410
thought as to who would be reading them.

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:58.100
But this concept was not unique to the world
of the internet.

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:03.330
In 1864, many British politicians were deeply
irritated by a telegram many of them received,

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:08.950
a message from Messrs Gabriel of 27 Harley
Street, that gave the opening times of their

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:11.440
dental practise, hoping for more business.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:16.950
But none of them would bite and we’ve not
smiled kindly on spam ever since.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:21.709
That’s about it for today, I need to go
now as I must reply to Mister Jakande’s

00:10:21.709 --> 00:10:27.160
email, he says there’s $6 million waiting
for me in a trust account, and all he needs

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:33.860
from me to release the funds is a small deposit
of 1%, I can’t miss this opportunity.

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:46.250
Thanks for 

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:52.980
the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

